  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1145 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nShowers and embedded thunderstorms will exit the region early\nTuesday morning as a low pressure system lifts northeast through\nthe western Great Lakes. Behind this departing low temperatures\nwill be a lot cooler Tuesday; ranging from around 60 across the\nnorth to around 70 across east-central Wisconsin. Although the\nweather is mainly expected to be dry until the next system arrives\nlater in the week, a weak mid level shortwave will bring a slight\nchance (10-20%) for light showers Tuesday night.\n\nA larger phasing system is expected towards the end of the week \nas southern and northern streams merge. Phasing systems generally \nhave much uncertainty in terms of timing but some general \nagreement has come into alignment with the most recent forecast \ncycles. Rain will start sometime in the late morning to afternoon \nhours on Thursday then cross the region into the overnight hours.\nHow long the system remains around on Friday remains uncertain \nand will depend largely on whether the phasing occurs overhead or \nto our east. After some possible showers Friday morning, things\nlook to dry out at least by Friday afternoon. Recent model runs have\nbacked off a bit on the snowflakes as the system winds down. This\nsystem will also bring a period of windy conditions for Friday,\nespecially across the Door Peninsula.\n\nBehind this departing low a large area of high pressure will build\nback into the region, which will bring dry weather for the weekend\ninto early next week. The high will also allow temperatures to\nslowly warm through early next week, as highs recover back into\nthe 60s.",
